{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-126765",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "First, we separate the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2x\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}}}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int 2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{arcsin}⁡y & ={x}^{2}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a perfectly valid general solution. However, if we want to write $y=y(x)$ explicitly, then we can solve for $y.$ This gives\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}+C)\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary constant.",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
